-#
  One cell's markup, driven by a cell descriptor from `LogsHelper#log_row_cell`.

  `kind` decides the element, so the Ruby side owns the formatting rules and the JS side
  owns nothing but rebuilding these same elements. Keep this in step with `cellHtml` in
  `logs_explorer_controller.js`; the two are the paired halves of "a live row looks like a
  loaded row".

-# locals: (cell:)

- case cell[:kind]
- when :severity
  -#
    Nothing but the value: the colour and width live on the <td> itself (see
    `log-severity-cell` in _dawa.scss), so there is no inner element to inset it or add
    padding. The level is not visible text, so it rides in the title for discoverability
    and remains in the message cell's field list. An unknown level stays neutral rather than
    being omitted, so every row keeps the same column widths.
  -#
    NOTE: an empty span is emitted deliberately. Haml needs *something* here, and `cellHtml`
    in the JS builds the same shape; the <td> already carries the color class.
  %span.d-none{title: cell[:value].presence || t("logs.index.none"), data: {bs_toggle: "tooltip"}}
- when :time
  %span.text-nowrap= cell[:value]
- when :message
  -#
    The message is its own disclosure: the summary is the one-line preview, and the body is
    the whole record. `<details>` gives the click-to-expand for free, with no JS and no state
    to keep in step -- so a live-appended row behaves like a loaded one.

    The body leads with `_msg` on its own, unlabelled and at the table's normal font size:
    it is the line the row exists to show, not one field among many, so it reads as the
    message continued rather than as a labelled entry. The remaining fields follow as a list
    of "key: value" rows, which is quicker to scan than braces and quotes. `list-unstyled`
    drops the bullets; only the list is `.small`, since it is supporting metadata.

    The summary text is wrapped in a span so the stylesheet can hide JUST the text while the
    disclosure is open (the body repeats it in full, so showing both prints the line twice).

    Collapsing is driven by clicking the body, not the summary. Once the summary text is
    hidden the summary is an empty strip, which is an invisible and undiscoverable hit target;
    `logs_explorer#collapseMessage` on the body closes the disclosure instead. The summary
    stays as the control for keyboard and assistive tech, which is what `<details>` provides
    natively.
  %details.log-message
    %summary.text-truncate
      %span.log-message-summary-text= cell[:value]
    .mb-3{data: {action: "click->logs-explorer#collapseMessage"}}= cell[:message]
    %ul.list-unstyled.small.mb-0{data: {action: "click->logs-explorer#collapseMessage"}}
      - cell[:fields].each do |field|
        %li
          %i= field[:key]
          \: #{field[:value]}
- when :badge
  %span.badge.text-bg-light= cell[:value]
- when :none
  %span.text-muted= cell[:value]
